WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
A public agency can work hard, employ
committed people, and still miss something

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:05.880
essential about the lives it affects.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.420
Minnesota has already faced that
challenge.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.620
In its 2014 report on advancing health
equity, the Minnesota Department of

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.620
Health described persistent inequities
despite years of effort.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.520
It named structural racism as a
contributor and recommended changes within

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.240
institutions, including its own grant
making and community relationships.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.160
The date of this report matters.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.420
We are using a documented moment of
institutional self-examination, not

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.500
presenting its statistics as today's
conditions.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.520
Consider the shift in the question.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.100
If we begin by asking why some people do
not benefit from what we offer, we may

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.900
focus on their knowledge, behavior, or
willingness to participate.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.180
If we also ask how our institution defines
the problem, chooses its partners,

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.900
and distributes resources, a different set
of explanations becomes visible.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.320
Neither question can be settled by good
intentions.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.840
Both require evidence, and the people
affected must help determine what evidence

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.940
matters. That is where this course begins:
with an institution willing to

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.440
examine its own view of the world.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.440
For staff and leaders, the challenge is to
connect that examination to decisions

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.680
that change people's experience of
service, opportunity, and belonging.

00:01:09.375 --> 00:01:13.135
Culture includes the expectations we learn
about respect, responsibility,

00:01:13.495 --> 00:01:17.195
authority, family, time, achievement, and
what makes a life worthwhile.

00:01:18.115 --> 00:01:19.675
Some expectations are easy to name.

00:01:20.295 --> 00:01:22.255
Others disappear into the way things are
normally done.

00:01:22.775 --> 00:01:26.615
When an institution builds those
expectations into its routines, people who

00:01:26.615 --> 00:01:29.495
already understand them have less
explaining and adapting to do.

00:01:29.935 --> 00:01:33.275
In this course, dominant culture means the
cultural assumptions with enough

00:01:33.275 --> 00:01:35.415
institutional authority to shape the
standard.

00:01:35.415 --> 00:01:39.215
In Minnesota, we are examining the
historical influence of white majority

00:01:39.215 --> 00:01:42.935
culture alongside American traditions,
professional practices, and the

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:44.855
particular habits of public organizations.

00:01:45.535 --> 00:01:47.835
These influences overlap. They are not
identical.

00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:50.495
White Minnesotans are not a single
cultural group.

00:01:51.135 --> 00:01:52.935
Race does not tell us how any colleague
thinks.

00:01:53.475 --> 00:01:57.675
People of color may also work within,
reinforce, challenge, or reshape an

00:01:57.675 --> 00:01:59.075
institution's prevailing norms.

00:01:59.075 --> 00:02:04.135
Disability, generation, income, geography,
faith, and personal history

00:02:04.135 --> 00:02:05.575
complicate every simple picture.

00:02:06.175 --> 00:02:08.555
The point is to make the standard visible
enough to examine.

00:02:09.215 --> 00:02:10.595
What does this organization reward?

00:02:11.055 --> 00:02:12.295
What counts as credible knowledge?

00:02:12.755 --> 00:02:14.575
Who must change their manner of speaking
to be heard?

00:02:15.075 --> 00:02:17.575
Who is expected to manage a difficult
process without help?

00:02:18.075 --> 00:02:19.955
These are questions about how authority
operates.

00:02:20.435 --> 00:02:22.515
They cannot be answered by looking at the
faces in a room.

00:02:25.958 --> 00:02:30.058
Imagine reviewing a service that staff
describe as accessible because everyone

00:02:30.058 --> 00:02:33.498
receives the same offer. That statement
tells us something about delivery.

00:02:33.978 --> 00:02:37.638
It does not yet tell us whether people
understood the offer, could use it, found

00:02:37.638 --> 00:02:39.918
it relevant, or could influence what was
offered.

00:02:40.418 --> 00:02:43.258
Those are different questions, and each
may require different evidence.

00:02:43.798 --> 00:02:45.898
We also need to avoid the opposite
shortcut.

00:02:46.418 --> 00:02:50.278
A disappointing outcome does not, by
itself, tell us that cultural bias caused

00:02:50.278 --> 00:02:54.478
it. We must examine the pathway, hear how
people experienced it, and test

00:02:54.478 --> 00:02:55.838
explanations against the facts.

00:02:56.258 --> 00:02:59.218
The goal is disciplined inquiry followed
by a responsible response.

00:02:59.758 --> 00:03:02.598
Throughout this course, we will move among
history, community perspectives,

00:03:02.878 --> 00:03:04.778
workplace experience, and service
decisions.

00:03:05.358 --> 00:03:08.598
We will distinguish a documented finding
from an interpretation, and an

00:03:08.598 --> 00:03:10.698
interpretation from something we still
need to ask.

00:03:11.118 --> 00:03:13.438
We will also identify who has the
authority to act.

00:03:13.838 --> 00:03:15.918
Keep one real responsibility in mind as
you watch.

00:03:16.398 --> 00:03:19.478
It might involve helping someone
understand options, supervising a team,

00:03:19.638 --> 00:03:23.278
designing a program, studying outcomes, or
choosing how resources are used.

00:03:23.778 --> 00:03:26.258
Ask where your role gives you influence
and where you need others.

00:03:26.758 --> 00:03:29.198
The Water We Swim In introduces a larger
learning journey.

00:03:29.658 --> 00:03:32.998
Its value will depend on what you can
notice, investigate, and change after the

00:03:32.998 --> 00:03:33.558
video ends.

00:03:34.125 --> 00:03:37.985
American public life contains powerful
commitments to individual rights,

00:03:38.325 --> 00:03:40.485
opportunity, personal agency, and
achievement.

00:03:41.105 --> 00:03:45.165
It also contains arguments about whose
rights count, how opportunity is created,

00:03:45.385 --> 00:03:47.005
and what society owes its members.

00:03:47.685 --> 00:03:51.025
The American cultural brief is useful
because it makes that inheritance

00:03:51.025 --> 00:03:52.265
available for examination.

00:03:53.105 --> 00:03:54.985
It is not a description of every American.

00:03:55.425 --> 00:03:59.625
Take the idea of merit. A job should be
awarded for relevant ability rather than

00:03:59.625 --> 00:04:02.645
personal connections. That principle can
protect fairness.

00:04:03.265 --> 00:04:06.625
But the institution still has to decide
how ability will be recognized.

00:04:07.325 --> 00:04:11.045
Does it rely on credentials, a particular
interview style, experience in

00:04:11.045 --> 00:04:14.765
familiar organizations, a work sample, or
evidence from several sources?

00:04:15.465 --> 00:04:18.765
Each choice makes some knowledge easier to
demonstrate and other knowledge

00:04:18.765 --> 00:04:22.445
harder to see. The same distinction
applies to personal responsibility.

00:04:23.505 --> 00:04:26.745
Responsibility may involve asking for
help, supporting others, or working

00:04:26.745 --> 00:04:27.725
through a shared decision.

00:04:27.725 --> 00:04:32.465
If we recognize it only as acting alone
and confidently navigating a system, we

00:04:32.465 --> 00:04:35.445
may mistake our preferred expression of
the value for the value itself.

00:04:36.245 --> 00:04:38.985
Our task is not to discard principles
whenever people differ.

00:04:39.365 --> 00:04:43.025
It is to examine how a principle becomes a
rule, and whether the rule actually

00:04:43.025 --> 00:04:47.485
serves its purpose. That requires clear
criteria, evidence, and willingness to

00:04:47.485 --> 00:04:48.245
revise an assumption.

00:04:48.833 --> 00:04:53.333
Consider a familiar American promise:
people should have a fair opportunity to

00:04:53.333 --> 00:04:57.193
build a secure life. Housing offers a
concrete example of why that promise

00:04:57.193 --> 00:04:58.993
requires institutional examination.

00:04:59.613 --> 00:05:02.853
The University of Minnesota's Mapping
Prejudice project documents property

00:05:02.853 --> 00:05:06.393
restrictions that barred people who were
not white from buying or occupying

00:05:06.393 --> 00:05:10.453
homes. Such restrictions helped organize
access to neighborhoods through rules,

00:05:10.813 --> 00:05:12.393
not simply through individual choices.

00:05:12.873 --> 00:05:14.813
The connection to public service is
analytical.

00:05:14.813 --> 00:05:18.413
A system can tell people to make
responsible choices while the available

00:05:18.413 --> 00:05:20.753
choices have already been shaped by
earlier decisions.

00:05:21.313 --> 00:05:25.133
We need to examine both agency and
opportunity: what a person wants to do,

00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:26.753
and what conditions make that possible.

00:05:27.413 --> 00:05:28.753
One cannot be read from the other.

00:05:29.193 --> 00:05:31.233
This history also changes how we discuss
merit.

00:05:31.733 --> 00:05:34.973
An achievement can reflect effort and
ability while drawing on opportunities

00:05:34.973 --> 00:05:36.513
that were distributed unequally.

00:05:37.013 --> 00:05:39.033
Recognizing that does not erase the
person's work.

00:05:39.253 --> 00:05:42.593
It challenges us to examine the pathway
before assuming that the outcome is a

00:05:42.593 --> 00:05:44.313
complete measure of character or
capability.

00:05:45.413 --> 00:05:49.173
For staff, the practical discipline is to
resist a quick moral explanation for

00:05:49.173 --> 00:05:50.293
another person's situation.

00:05:50.853 --> 00:05:54.133
For leaders, it includes examining how
rules, partnerships, and resource

00:05:54.133 --> 00:05:55.493
decisions shape opportunity.

00:05:55.953 --> 00:05:59.493
A cultural lens becomes consequential when
we can identify what the organization

00:05:59.493 --> 00:05:59.973
controls.

00:06:03.667 --> 00:06:06.807
A broad cultural description can help us
notice a question.

00:06:07.427 --> 00:06:09.607
It cannot answer that question for an
individual.

00:06:10.207 --> 00:06:14.327
The United States contains different
regional histories, languages, religions,

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:16.827
family arrangements, and relationships
with government.

00:06:17.387 --> 00:06:21.407
People also belong to professional,
disability, workplace, and generational

00:06:21.407 --> 00:06:24.767
cultures. They may draw on these
differently in different situations.

00:06:25.367 --> 00:06:28.087
This is why a national average is not a
person's biography.

00:06:28.727 --> 00:06:32.447
If a framework describes individualism, it
does not tell us whether a particular

00:06:32.447 --> 00:06:34.187
American prefers to decide alone.

00:06:34.767 --> 00:06:38.487
If a community brief describes collective
responsibility, it does not tell us

00:06:38.487 --> 00:06:39.587
who should speak for an adult.

00:06:40.207 --> 00:06:43.007
Treating a description as a prediction
creates another barrier while appearing

00:06:43.007 --> 00:06:46.267
culturally informed. Use the American
brief first as a mirror.

00:06:46.887 --> 00:06:50.567
Which assumptions about success, time,
help, and authority do you bring to work?

00:06:51.367 --> 00:06:54.127
Then consider how your organization
reinforces some of them.

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:56.387
A deadline may have a legitimate purpose.

00:06:56.927 --> 00:06:59.367
A preferred communication style may simply
be familiar.

00:06:59.967 --> 00:07:01.387
Those require different responses.

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:05.327
For a decision you know well, identify the
purpose of the standard, the evidence

00:07:05.327 --> 00:07:08.627
that supports it, and the adaptations that
could preserve the purpose while

00:07:08.627 --> 00:07:11.407
improving access. Some constraints will
remain.

00:07:11.607 --> 00:07:15.087
Explain them honestly. Cultural competence
includes judgment about what can

00:07:15.087 --> 00:07:18.227
change, what must be protected, and what
you have not yet understood.

00:07:18.792 --> 00:07:23.012
Minnesota's public identity often
emphasizes cooperation, civic duty,

00:07:23.392 --> 00:07:26.092
helpfulness, and confidence that people
can solve problems together.

00:07:26.752 --> 00:07:29.952
The Minnesota cultural brief traces parts
of that identity through settlement,

00:07:30.132 --> 00:07:32.612
churches, farms, cooperatives, and
political organizing.

00:07:33.332 --> 00:07:36.912
These are historical threads, not a single
explanation for every Minnesotan's

00:07:36.912 --> 00:07:39.432
behavior. One useful example is the
cooperative.

00:07:40.112 --> 00:07:43.272
People pool resources, share risk, and
make decisions about a common

00:07:43.272 --> 00:07:46.932
undertaking. That tradition offers more
than a pleasant story about community.

00:07:47.292 --> 00:07:49.032
It raises a question about membership.

00:07:49.552 --> 00:07:52.552
Who can participate, who sets the terms,
and whose needs shape what the

00:07:52.552 --> 00:07:56.532
organization does? The question matters
because being offered help is not the

00:07:56.532 --> 00:07:58.872
same as belonging to the group that
decides how help works.

00:07:59.332 --> 00:08:02.392
An institution may take pride in serving
people while retaining all the

00:08:02.392 --> 00:08:03.732
authority to define their needs.

00:08:04.252 --> 00:08:07.652
It may welcome participation after the
important choices have already been made.

00:08:08.092 --> 00:08:09.552
Keep the strength and examine its reach.

00:08:10.412 --> 00:08:13.472
Cooperation can support culturally
responsive work when people affected help

00:08:13.472 --> 00:08:14.832
shape the problem and the response.

00:08:14.832 --> 00:08:17.972
It can also leave an established circle
comfortable with itself.

00:08:18.492 --> 00:08:21.612
The distinction is visible in whose
knowledge changes a decision, whose

00:08:21.612 --> 00:08:24.772
priorities receive resources, and whether
people can challenge the arrangement

00:08:24.772 --> 00:08:26.412
without losing their standing within it.

00:08:26.792 --> 00:08:30.732
A familiar account of Minnesota can begin
with European settlement and end with

00:08:30.732 --> 00:08:32.052
a successful civic tradition.

00:08:32.672 --> 00:08:33.652
That account is incomplete.

00:08:34.212 --> 00:08:37.612
The land, institutions, and opportunities
in the story were shaped by unequal

00:08:37.612 --> 00:08:42.172
power. The source course identifies Native
dispossession, racial exclusion, and

00:08:42.172 --> 00:08:45.532
histories of community resistance that
cannot be treated as footnotes to the

00:08:45.532 --> 00:08:49.532
majority's experience. Tribal Nations also
require a distinct understanding.

00:08:50.332 --> 00:08:53.112
Their relationship with the state involves
sovereignty and specific

00:08:53.112 --> 00:08:55.632
responsibilities, not only cultural
difference.

00:08:56.172 --> 00:08:59.492
Learning about culture does not replace
the appropriate Tribal relationships or

00:08:59.492 --> 00:09:03.132
official processes. The application should
help staff find those connections

00:09:03.312 --> 00:09:05.572
rather than blend them into a general list
of communities.

00:09:06.412 --> 00:09:09.652
Other Minnesotans have long contributed to
the state's institutions while being

00:09:09.652 --> 00:09:11.492
left outside its favored picture of
itself.

00:09:12.152 --> 00:09:15.352
When we make belonging depend on fitting
an inherited image, we can overlook

00:09:15.352 --> 00:09:17.132
people who are already shaping Minnesota.

00:09:17.872 --> 00:09:20.872
The cultural briefs should expand our
historical field of view, including

00:09:20.872 --> 00:09:23.952
variation within communities and the
knowledge produced by their organizations.

00:09:24.772 --> 00:09:26.472
This changes the question we ask at work.

00:09:26.912 --> 00:09:30.112
Instead of treating an unfamiliar
perspective as an addition to an otherwise

00:09:30.112 --> 00:09:33.192
complete account, we consider whether the
account was incomplete from the

00:09:33.192 --> 00:09:36.512
beginning. That is a substantive reason to
learn from communities before

00:09:36.512 --> 00:09:37.792
deciding what the problem is.

00:09:38.375 --> 00:09:40.955
How would an organization know whether
people belong?

00:09:41.835 --> 00:09:44.015
Friendly interactions provide one kind of
information.

00:09:44.775 --> 00:09:46.315
Attendance at an event provides another.

00:09:46.955 --> 00:09:50.435
Neither tells us whether someone can
influence a consequential decision, bring

00:09:50.435 --> 00:09:54.095
relevant knowledge into their work, or
receive support without being treated as

00:09:54.095 --> 00:09:57.715
an exception. Consider two ways of
reviewing a workplace initiative.

00:09:58.275 --> 00:10:00.675
One asks whether the activity was offered
to everyone.

00:10:01.135 --> 00:10:04.735
The other also asks who could use it,
whose contributions were taken seriously,

00:10:05.095 --> 00:10:06.055
and what happened afterward.

00:10:06.055 --> 00:10:08.915
These questions do not presume the
initiative failed.

00:10:09.515 --> 00:10:12.295
They make success more specific and more
open to examination.

00:10:13.115 --> 00:10:14.815
The same reasoning applies to a service.

00:10:15.395 --> 00:10:19.375
A person's experience may include cultural
expectations, language, disability

00:10:19.375 --> 00:10:23.455
access, time, money, geography, and
previous encounters with institutions.

00:10:24.235 --> 00:10:26.055
We should not call every difficulty
cultural.

00:10:26.575 --> 00:10:28.995
Nor should we ignore culture because other
conditions matter.

00:10:29.435 --> 00:10:32.455
We need enough detail to understand how
these factors interact in this

00:10:32.455 --> 00:10:36.435
situation. Choose one claim your team
makes about its work: welcoming,

00:10:36.575 --> 00:10:38.695
accessible, person-centered, or fair.

00:10:39.315 --> 00:10:41.395
What observable experience would support
that claim?

00:10:41.735 --> 00:10:42.875
Whose account could challenge it?

00:10:43.255 --> 00:10:45.095
What could the team change if the evidence
does?

00:10:45.655 --> 00:10:48.615
Minnesota's civic commitment becomes more
useful when it supports those

00:10:48.615 --> 00:10:52.975
questions. Pride in public service can
then motivate an honest examination of

00:10:52.975 --> 00:10:53.875
who benefits and how.

00:10:57.292 --> 00:11:00.652
The American brief makes a distinction
that deserves more attention.

00:11:01.272 --> 00:11:04.612
It describes mainstream ideas embedded in
professional training and public

00:11:04.612 --> 00:11:07.272
systems, rather than a single community of
people.

00:11:07.832 --> 00:11:10.432
Its purpose is to make that institutional
frame visible.

00:11:10.952 --> 00:11:14.552
The Minnesota brief similarly asks staff
to examine the state's systems and

00:11:14.552 --> 00:11:17.212
habits, while recognizing differences
within the state.

00:11:17.652 --> 00:11:21.132
For this course, examining the
historically dominant white cultural lens

00:11:21.132 --> 00:11:23.872
means examining the authority of familiar
expectations.

00:11:24.492 --> 00:11:27.832
A practice does not become culturally
neutral because it is widely used,

00:11:27.932 --> 00:11:31.152
expressed professionally, or followed by
employees from many backgrounds.

00:11:31.632 --> 00:11:35.272
We still need to ask how it defines a
reasonable request, credible knowledge, or

00:11:35.272 --> 00:11:39.112
a successful service. That examination is
different from assigning a motive to

00:11:39.112 --> 00:11:43.152
an individual. We can investigate who
benefits from a standard without claiming

00:11:43.152 --> 00:11:45.272
that every person applying it intended
exclusion.

00:11:45.892 --> 00:11:49.192
We can also recognize an employee's
sincere commitment while asking whether

00:11:49.192 --> 00:11:50.452
the practice needs to change.

00:11:50.752 --> 00:11:53.832
The important distinction is between an
explanation and an exemption.

00:11:54.552 --> 00:11:57.072
Familiarity may explain why a practice
escaped notice.

00:11:57.592 --> 00:11:59.312
It does not exempt the practice from
review.

00:11:59.892 --> 00:12:03.232
Equally, identifying a cultural influence
does not establish that the practice

00:12:03.232 --> 00:12:07.312
is wrong. The work is to examine its
purpose, consequences, and alternatives

00:12:07.312 --> 00:12:08.352
with the people it affects.

00:12:08.958 --> 00:12:12.518
The Minnesota brief presents civic
participation as a source of public

00:12:12.518 --> 00:12:16.658
strength. For human services, the deeper
question is what participation permits

00:12:16.658 --> 00:12:20.738
people to do. Being welcomed, being asked
for information, and helping define a

00:12:20.738 --> 00:12:22.918
problem are different relationships with
an institution.

00:12:23.618 --> 00:12:27.298
When the institution defines the problem
first, community members may be invited

00:12:27.298 --> 00:12:28.978
to explain how they fit its categories.

00:12:29.678 --> 00:12:32.398
When their knowledge can change the
question, the institution may discover

00:12:32.398 --> 00:12:34.218
that its original account was incomplete.

00:12:34.778 --> 00:12:38.418
This distinction concerns the design of
participation, not how warmly an event

00:12:38.418 --> 00:12:42.078
is conducted. Minnesota's Department of
Health addressed this directly in its

00:12:42.078 --> 00:12:45.958
twenty fourteen report. It recommended
meaningful community participation in

00:12:45.958 --> 00:12:48.938
project governance and oversight,
including ways to consider conflicting

00:12:48.938 --> 00:12:52.878
advice. This was a recommendation about
institutional decision making, not

00:12:52.878 --> 00:12:55.878
merely attendance. For our work,
disagreement therefore matters.

00:12:56.358 --> 00:12:59.578
A community partner may agree with a
public purpose while questioning the

00:12:59.578 --> 00:13:00.998
agency's definition of success.

00:13:01.658 --> 00:13:03.498
Different community members may also
disagree.

00:13:04.138 --> 00:13:07.278
The organization needs a way to hear those
differences without selecting only

00:13:07.278 --> 00:13:09.178
the account that confirms its preferred
approach.

00:13:09.898 --> 00:13:12.898
Belonging becomes more substantial when
people can contribute knowledge that

00:13:12.898 --> 00:13:16.638
changes the analysis, understand how a
decision was reached, and challenge an

00:13:16.638 --> 00:13:20.498
inadequate response. The learning question
is where those opportunities actually

00:13:20.498 --> 00:13:20.858
exist.

00:13:21.458 --> 00:13:25.058
History gives this inquiry depth, but it
also requires discipline.

00:13:25.678 --> 00:13:29.318
The Minnesota brief places accounts of
civic achievement beside exclusion and

00:13:29.318 --> 00:13:32.538
unequal opportunity. Those histories
belong in the same frame.

00:13:33.198 --> 00:13:36.118
A description of the public good is
incomplete if it leaves out who could

00:13:36.118 --> 00:13:38.038
influence its meaning or share in its
benefits.

00:13:38.738 --> 00:13:40.158
There are several different questions
here.

00:13:40.498 --> 00:13:41.338
What happened historically?

00:13:41.938 --> 00:13:44.538
Which institutional arrangements carried
consequences forward?

00:13:44.978 --> 00:13:46.278
What conditions are present now?

00:13:46.698 --> 00:13:49.118
How does this particular person understand
their experience?

00:13:49.118 --> 00:13:52.618
Evidence for one question does not
automatically answer the others.

00:13:52.998 --> 00:13:56.998
For example, a documented history of
exclusion can justify examining how an

00:13:56.998 --> 00:14:00.618
institution earns trust. It cannot tell us
that every person from an affected

00:14:00.618 --> 00:14:02.838
community distrusts every public employee.

00:14:03.298 --> 00:14:06.618
Nor can a positive encounter establish
that the effects of earlier exclusion

00:14:06.618 --> 00:14:09.958
have disappeared. The Department of
Health's twenty fourteen report also

00:14:09.958 --> 00:14:13.798
cautioned against a deficit account of
communities, urging recognition of their

00:14:13.798 --> 00:14:16.118
strengths and contributions alongside
inequities.

00:14:16.118 --> 00:14:20.038
That matters for our visual and
intellectual attention: people contribute

00:14:20.038 --> 00:14:22.478
knowledge, organize change, and shape
public life.

00:14:22.958 --> 00:14:25.798
As we turn to the highway history, keep
both responsibilities in view.

00:14:26.278 --> 00:14:28.978
Take institutional power and racial
exclusion seriously.

00:14:29.478 --> 00:14:32.678
Keep the evidence specific enough that
history opens questions rather than

00:14:32.678 --> 00:14:34.118
becoming a new way to predict people.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:37.770
A highway can be described as a route that
connects places.

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:42.130
For the people in its path, it can also
divide a neighborhood, remove homes, and

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:44.030
alter relationships built over
generations.

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:46.490
Both descriptions concern the same
project.

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:47.910
They count different things.

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:51.930
The Minnesota Historical Society describes
resistance to Interstate Ninety-four

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:54.790
in four Twin Cities neighborhoods during
the nineteen-fifties and

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:58.250
nineteen-sixties. Rondo residents
organized and negotiated.

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:01.610
So did residents of Merriam Park, Prospect
Park, and Seward.

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:04.870
The communities had different resources
and achieved different results.

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:08.030
This was not a story of some people caring
about their neighborhoods while

00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:09.210
others failed to participate.

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:13.490
In Rondo, a center of Black community life
in Saint Paul, hundreds of families

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:17.150
lost their homes. Merriam Park residents
drew on connections to influential

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:19.870
institutions and elected officials while
pressing for changes.

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:24.330
The historical account identifies racial
and class bias and unequal political

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:25.830
influence as part of the comparison.

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:27.770
Keep the scale and setting clear.

00:15:27.770 --> 00:15:31.470
This is a history of highway planning, not
a finding about today's Department of

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:35.070
Human Services. We are studying it because
public decisions reveal whose

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:38.790
knowledge carries weight. The question
travels across institutions even when the

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:42.490
particular powers, obligations, people,
and available remedies are different.

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:45.490
That distinction keeps the comparison
useful and honest.

00:15:46.250 --> 00:15:49.890
A public process can measure participation
without measuring influence.

00:15:50.650 --> 00:15:53.950
It can count comments while leaving
unchanged the criteria that determine

00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:58.050
which comments matter. It can recognize an
organized concern and still give

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:02.590
greater weight to a familiar institution,
a technical vocabulary, or a proposal

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:04.690
that fits a decision already taking shape.

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:08.270
That is why cultural competence in an
organization involves more than

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:09.950
understanding interpersonal style.

00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:12.830
It includes examining how expertise is
recognized.

00:16:12.830 --> 00:16:15.730
A resident may know what a change will do
to daily life.

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:18.310
A professional may understand the formal
options.

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:20.770
Neither kind of knowledge is complete by
itself.

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:24.710
The institution decides how they meet and
what authority each receives.

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:26.950
Consider a current decision in your work.

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:30.470
At what point can the people affected
influence its purpose or alternatives?

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:34.650
If they are invited only after the central
choices are settled, what can they

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:38.310
realistically change? Does the
organization explain that limit, or does

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:40.970
participation appear to promise more
influence than it offers?

00:16:40.970 --> 00:16:45.070
These questions do not remove legal
responsibilities or technical constraints.

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:48.730
They make the boundaries visible and open
the remaining choices to meaningful

00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:52.730
contribution. People should be able to
understand which decisions are theirs,

00:16:53.210 --> 00:16:56.970
which belong to the agency, what evidence
is being used, and how a disagreement

00:16:56.970 --> 00:16:58.450
can receive a substantive response.

00:17:02.208 --> 00:17:06.188
Suppose a team discovers that people have
been consulted repeatedly but cannot

00:17:06.188 --> 00:17:07.788
identify anything that changed.

00:17:08.208 --> 00:17:11.108
An immediate response might be to explain
the process more clearly.

00:17:11.368 --> 00:17:13.908
That can help, but it may not address the
actual concern.

00:17:14.468 --> 00:17:18.208
The problem might be that the team has no
decision authority, that no resources

00:17:18.208 --> 00:17:21.828
were assigned, or that community knowledge
never entered the criteria for

00:17:21.828 --> 00:17:25.468
choosing an option. A stronger review
separates those possibilities.

00:17:26.068 --> 00:17:28.368
What was promised? What decisions remained
open?

00:17:28.788 --> 00:17:29.828
Who received the information?

00:17:30.428 --> 00:17:32.248
What changed, what did not, and why?

00:17:32.948 --> 00:17:36.668
If the same obstacle recurs, who can
change the arrangement rather than asking

00:17:36.668 --> 00:17:38.228
participants to repeat their stories?

00:17:38.928 --> 00:17:41.028
This is where staff and leader
responsibilities meet.

00:17:41.708 --> 00:17:44.428
Staff can identify a pattern and preserve
what people actually said.

00:17:45.188 --> 00:17:48.628
Leaders can examine authority, priorities,
resources, and the consequences of

00:17:48.628 --> 00:17:49.668
leaving the pattern in place.

00:17:50.368 --> 00:17:53.368
Both need to return to the people affected
with a clear account of the response,

00:17:53.568 --> 00:17:57.428
including its limits. Before we move on,
think about one decision you can

00:17:57.428 --> 00:18:01.068
influence. Name the people whose lives or
work it affects, the point at which

00:18:01.068 --> 00:18:03.648
their knowledge could change the decision,
and who controls that point.

00:18:04.128 --> 00:18:07.368
The next chapters will examine what
evidence can tell us, how people define

00:18:07.368 --> 00:18:10.908
meaningful outcomes, and how to respond
without substituting an assumption for

00:18:10.908 --> 00:18:11.548
their experience.

00:18:12.208 --> 00:18:14.468
Now bring the question into human
services.

00:18:15.028 --> 00:18:18.588
When participation in a service differs
across groups, what do we think that

00:18:18.588 --> 00:18:22.728
difference means? A familiar explanation
can arrive before we have examined the

00:18:22.728 --> 00:18:25.408
evidence. Perhaps a group prefers family
care.

00:18:25.808 --> 00:18:27.868
Perhaps people do not know the service
exists.

00:18:28.608 --> 00:18:30.928
Perhaps the service is unavailable where
they live.

00:18:31.388 --> 00:18:34.288
Perhaps previous encounters have made the
offer difficult to trust.

00:18:34.648 --> 00:18:35.768
These are different explanations.

00:18:36.248 --> 00:18:37.408
They imply different actions.

00:18:37.408 --> 00:18:42.328
A 2021 study prepared for Minnesota DHS
compared racial and ethnic groups in

00:18:42.328 --> 00:18:45.388
level of care screenings and use of home
and community based services.

00:18:46.068 --> 00:18:48.028
Its administrative records described
patterns.

00:18:48.508 --> 00:18:51.628
The authors explicitly said the data could
not establish their causes.

00:18:52.168 --> 00:18:55.468
The categories also concealed important
differences among people placed in the

00:18:55.468 --> 00:18:58.368
same racial group. That limitation is part
of the teaching.

00:18:58.968 --> 00:19:01.288
A service record can tell us that
something was used.

00:19:01.608 --> 00:19:05.048
It may tell us much less about whether the
person understood the choices, could

00:19:05.048 --> 00:19:08.588
find a provider, felt respected, or
received support that fitted their life.

00:19:09.168 --> 00:19:10.488
Notice the two possible mistakes.

00:19:10.948 --> 00:19:13.128
We can treat a difference as proof of a
cultural preference.

00:19:13.528 --> 00:19:16.488
We can also treat it as proof of one
specific institutional cause.

00:19:17.088 --> 00:19:19.488
Both conclusions move beyond what these
records establish.

00:19:20.168 --> 00:19:23.728
A responsible explanation has to remain
open to evidence that could change it.

00:19:24.125 --> 00:19:27.265
The companion stakeholder study brings
another kind of evidence.

00:19:27.965 --> 00:19:31.665
Community advisory board members described
problems with information, language

00:19:31.665 --> 00:19:34.505
access, service fit, and relationships
with the system.

00:19:35.005 --> 00:19:37.085
They also challenged broad racial
categories.

00:19:37.585 --> 00:19:41.005
Their accounts identify experiences and
possible mechanisms that administrative

00:19:41.005 --> 00:19:42.565
data cannot show by themselves.

00:19:43.125 --> 00:19:46.145
They do not tell us how common every
experience is across Minnesota.

00:19:46.625 --> 00:19:48.145
Put the two forms of evidence together.

00:19:48.785 --> 00:19:51.325
Suppose records show lower use of a
particular support.

00:19:51.745 --> 00:19:55.245
Before deciding that the community does
not want it, examine the path between

00:19:55.245 --> 00:19:56.885
needing support and receiving it.

00:19:57.265 --> 00:20:00.005
Was the offer understood? Was a suitable
provider available?

00:20:00.645 --> 00:20:02.445
Did the arrangement make participation
possible?

00:20:03.045 --> 00:20:06.145
Could the person refuse an unsuitable
option without losing the chance to

00:20:06.145 --> 00:20:10.305
discuss another? Those are questions for
investigation, not findings we already

00:20:10.305 --> 00:20:13.565
possess. Staff also know where people
repeatedly become stuck.

00:20:14.005 --> 00:20:16.165
That knowledge matters, but it has limits
too.

00:20:16.625 --> 00:20:18.745
A worker may see only those who reach the
office.

00:20:19.205 --> 00:20:22.245
A manager may see only the complaints that
survive an intimidating process.

00:20:22.245 --> 00:20:25.205
People who stop trying can disappear from
both accounts.

00:20:25.725 --> 00:20:28.765
A culturally responsive review therefore
needs more than a dashboard and more

00:20:28.765 --> 00:20:29.805
than one compelling story.

00:20:30.345 --> 00:20:33.625
It needs a way to compare patterns, hear
affected people, examine service

00:20:33.625 --> 00:20:35.145
conditions, and revise explanations.

00:20:35.865 --> 00:20:38.825
The point is to make the next decision
better grounded, rather than make the

00:20:38.825 --> 00:20:39.705
uncertainty disappear.

00:20:40.625 --> 00:20:42.205
Here is a judgment to make.

00:20:42.705 --> 00:20:45.365
A review team wants to respond to a
difference in service use.

00:20:45.985 --> 00:20:49.225
One recommendation is to improve outreach
about the existing support.

00:20:49.685 --> 00:20:52.385
Another is to invest in a more flexible
service arrangement.

00:20:52.885 --> 00:20:56.765
A third is to conduct a focused review
with affected people before selecting the

00:20:56.765 --> 00:21:00.365
larger investment, while correcting access
barriers already documented.

00:21:00.965 --> 00:21:03.525
Which recommendation can you justify with
the evidence available?

00:21:04.105 --> 00:21:05.385
Each has a plausible purpose.

00:21:06.045 --> 00:21:07.765
Outreach could address missing
information.

00:21:08.145 --> 00:21:10.805
A more flexible arrangement could improve
service fit.

00:21:11.205 --> 00:21:14.545
A focused review could distinguish those
problems, but it could also delay

00:21:14.545 --> 00:21:18.465
action. The difference in use alone cannot
tell us which investment will help.

00:21:18.685 --> 00:21:22.205
The recommendation needs to name the
barrier it addresses and the evidence that

00:21:22.205 --> 00:21:26.305
supports that choice. A time-bounded
review can be a defensible next step when

00:21:26.305 --> 00:21:28.025
the main explanation remains uncertain.

00:21:28.665 --> 00:21:30.705
It should not postpone correction of a
known barrier.

00:21:31.185 --> 00:21:34.585
Someone must identify which people are
missing, what evidence will distinguish

00:21:34.585 --> 00:21:36.805
the explanations, and who can change the
service.

00:21:37.405 --> 00:21:41.065
If the review only asks existing
participants, it may repeat the original

00:21:41.065 --> 00:21:44.725
blind spot. For a staff member, the next
step might be documenting the

00:21:44.725 --> 00:21:47.705
difference between what was offered and
what the person could actually use.

00:21:48.325 --> 00:21:51.805
For a leader, it might be assigning time
and authority to examine that pattern

00:21:51.805 --> 00:21:55.905
across cases. Both need a return path:
what did the affected people say

00:21:55.905 --> 00:21:57.385
improved, and what remained difficult?

00:21:57.885 --> 00:21:59.805
That is how evidence becomes accountable
action.

00:22:05.375 --> 00:22:06.935
Consider the word independent.

00:22:07.415 --> 00:22:08.715
It can mean directing your own life.

00:22:09.215 --> 00:22:12.655
It can also be used to mean doing things
without help, living alone, or making

00:22:12.655 --> 00:22:15.475
decisions privately. Those meanings are
not interchangeable.

00:22:16.115 --> 00:22:19.655
A person can need extensive assistance and
exercise strong authority over their

00:22:19.655 --> 00:22:23.355
life. A person can live alone and have
very little control over the choices

00:22:23.355 --> 00:22:27.255
available. This matters when professional
language becomes a picture of the good

00:22:27.255 --> 00:22:31.635
life. If a worker silently equates success
with moving away from family, a

00:22:31.635 --> 00:22:34.315
person who wants to remain close to
relatives may appear unambitious.

00:22:34.775 --> 00:22:38.275
If a worker assumes that a family should
decide because of the person's cultural

00:22:38.275 --> 00:22:40.895
background, the person's own preferences
may disappear.

00:22:41.495 --> 00:22:43.075
Both interpretations can sound caring.

00:22:43.535 --> 00:22:45.875
Both can replace the person's account with
someone else's.

00:22:46.235 --> 00:22:49.115
The disability rights history in this
collection is essential here.

00:22:49.475 --> 00:22:52.115
It challenges the assumption that
professionals always know best.

00:22:52.595 --> 00:22:55.335
The lesson is not to weaken
self-determination in the name of culture.

00:22:55.715 --> 00:22:58.975
It is to ask what self-determination looks
like for this person, with the

00:22:58.975 --> 00:23:02.815
support they choose. A useful starting
question is: what do you want to have

00:23:02.815 --> 00:23:06.935
more say over? That question can open
discussion of work, relationships, daily

00:23:06.935 --> 00:23:09.835
routines, privacy, belonging, or control
over support.

00:23:10.375 --> 00:23:13.415
The answer cannot be supplied by a
cultural profile or by a familiar

00:23:13.415 --> 00:23:14.635
professional success story.

00:23:15.042 --> 00:23:18.682
Here is a teaching example, not an account
of a particular family.

00:23:19.122 --> 00:23:21.802
An adult wants a relative involved in
discussing support.

00:23:22.262 --> 00:23:25.202
A practitioner worries that the relative
will influence the decision.

00:23:25.662 --> 00:23:28.822
That worry may deserve attention, but it
does not settle the question.

00:23:29.422 --> 00:23:32.962
Everyone makes decisions within
relationships, and professional advice is

00:23:32.962 --> 00:23:36.882
also a form of influence. The practitioner
needs to learn what role the person

00:23:36.882 --> 00:23:38.242
wants the relative to have.

00:23:38.642 --> 00:23:43.002
Is the relative helping explain unfamiliar
options, remembering past experience,

00:23:43.442 --> 00:23:46.222
translating in an informal way, or
speaking over the person?

00:23:46.862 --> 00:23:47.862
These are different functions.

00:23:48.442 --> 00:23:50.042
An interpreter may be needed separately.

00:23:51.042 --> 00:23:52.302
Communication support may need to change.

00:23:52.702 --> 00:23:55.642
The person may want some conversation
together and some in private.

00:23:56.162 --> 00:23:59.422
The same care applies when someone prefers
to decide without family involvement.

00:24:00.102 --> 00:24:03.662
A worker should not interpret that
preference as disloyalty or assume that

00:24:03.662 --> 00:24:05.262
the community brief predicts otherwise.

00:24:05.762 --> 00:24:09.002
Nor should a person have to demonstrate
that they are culturally typical before

00:24:09.002 --> 00:24:10.062
their choice is respected.

00:24:10.442 --> 00:24:13.382
If preferences conflict, the task becomes
more demanding.

00:24:13.982 --> 00:24:17.142
Clarify the person's wishes and the
relevant decision authority, use

00:24:17.142 --> 00:24:20.682
appropriate communication support, and
follow applicable safeguarding and

00:24:20.682 --> 00:24:24.622
program requirements. Avoid solving a
complex disagreement with a slogan about

00:24:24.622 --> 00:24:26.062
either independence or family.

00:24:26.982 --> 00:24:29.802
Cultural responsiveness requires careful
attention to the person's actual

00:24:29.802 --> 00:24:33.522
circumstances, including the possibility
that people within the same family

00:24:33.522 --> 00:24:35.262
understand those circumstances
differently.

00:24:35.833 --> 00:24:39.493
Now examine how the organization would
judge the quality of that encounter.

00:24:40.073 --> 00:24:43.333
A completed appointment does not show
whether the person understood the choices.

00:24:44.013 --> 00:24:47.033
A signed agreement does not show whether
the available choices fitted the

00:24:47.033 --> 00:24:50.833
person's life. A family member's
satisfaction does not replace the person's

00:24:50.833 --> 00:24:54.133
own view. These measures may tell us
something, but each leaves a different

00:24:54.133 --> 00:24:57.913
question unanswered. A stronger review
asks whether the person could understand

00:24:57.913 --> 00:25:01.433
and influence the decision, whether chosen
supporters helped in the role the

00:25:01.433 --> 00:25:03.893
person wanted, and whether the resulting
support was usable.

00:25:03.893 --> 00:25:06.893
It also asks what the practitioner could
not change.

00:25:07.353 --> 00:25:10.993
A well-conducted conversation can still
end with no suitable provider, an

00:25:10.993 --> 00:25:14.213
inflexible schedule, or options that do
not match the person's goals.

00:25:14.813 --> 00:25:18.273
That gap belongs in an organizational
review, not solely in a worker's

00:25:18.273 --> 00:25:21.913
reflection journal. Think of one outcome
your team routinely calls successful.

00:25:22.453 --> 00:25:25.813
Does it describe a completed service
activity, or a change that matters to the

00:25:25.813 --> 00:25:28.233
person? Pause if you want to write down
the difference.

00:25:28.813 --> 00:25:30.373
The next step is to connect the two.

00:25:30.373 --> 00:25:33.913
An organization needs reliable processes,
and it needs to know whether those

00:25:33.913 --> 00:25:35.413
processes support people's lives.

00:25:36.213 --> 00:25:39.513
Cultural learning helps staff recognize
when their own definition of success has

00:25:39.513 --> 00:25:43.433
become too narrow. Community knowledge and
direct conversation help reveal what

00:25:43.433 --> 00:25:44.693
the definition has left out.

00:25:48.208 --> 00:25:52.428
The two DHS studies give us a way to
examine service choices more precisely.

00:25:53.128 --> 00:25:54.108
Begin with who is represented.

00:25:54.768 --> 00:25:58.028
The twenty twenty one quantitative report
studied older people and people with

00:25:58.028 --> 00:26:00.488
disabilities within Minnesota's Medicaid
population.

00:26:01.008 --> 00:26:04.628
It used claims, screening information, and
related administrative records.

00:26:05.028 --> 00:26:08.968
Its population snapshot was from March
twenty nineteen; other analyses followed

00:26:08.968 --> 00:26:10.608
people over specified periods.

00:26:10.888 --> 00:26:13.828
That is different from a survey of all
Minnesotans, and different from

00:26:13.828 --> 00:26:16.188
interviews about why people wanted or
declined support.

00:26:16.188 --> 00:26:19.668
A record of use answers a question about
recorded participation.

00:26:20.288 --> 00:26:23.708
It does not, by itself, tell us what
someone understood, what alternatives were

00:26:23.708 --> 00:26:26.488
available, or whether the arrangement
respected their priorities.

00:26:27.048 --> 00:26:29.808
The report explicitly identifies those
evidentiary limits.

00:26:30.328 --> 00:26:33.528
Our practical implication is to state the
population and the measure before

00:26:33.528 --> 00:26:37.688
explaining a difference. Otherwise, a
narrow observation can quietly become a

00:26:37.688 --> 00:26:38.828
broad story about a community.

00:26:39.368 --> 00:26:40.948
Also examine the comparison itself.

00:26:41.428 --> 00:26:44.528
The group with greater use is not
automatically the standard everyone should

00:26:44.528 --> 00:26:47.928
match. Greater use might reflect different
needs or available arrangements;

00:26:48.628 --> 00:26:51.228
lower use does not establish a preference
for managing without help.

00:26:51.708 --> 00:26:53.448
Those possibilities require investigation.

00:26:54.048 --> 00:26:56.748
The question for human services is
therefore more demanding than whether

00:26:56.748 --> 00:27:00.788
participation increased: did people have
understandable, relevant options and a

00:27:00.788 --> 00:27:02.048
real opportunity to use them?

00:27:02.458 --> 00:27:06.278
The companion stakeholder report brings
the inquiry closer to daily life.

00:27:07.058 --> 00:27:10.038
Advisory members discussed cultural
relevance in residential services,

00:27:10.318 --> 00:27:11.658
including the importance of food.

00:27:12.218 --> 00:27:15.538
This was part of their account of service
fit, not a claim that everyone in a

00:27:15.538 --> 00:27:18.258
racial category wants the same meals or
living arrangement.

00:27:18.838 --> 00:27:20.298
Why does this matter beyond a menu?

00:27:20.678 --> 00:27:24.778
A service can satisfy an institution's
familiar definition of provision while

00:27:24.778 --> 00:27:27.578
asking the person to relinquish ordinary
sources of belonging.

00:27:28.098 --> 00:27:31.378
This raises a question about quality: it
should include what an arrangement

00:27:31.378 --> 00:27:34.758
permits people to retain and direct in
their lives, alongside its other

00:27:34.758 --> 00:27:37.678
legitimate requirements. That inquiry must
stay individual.

00:27:38.358 --> 00:27:41.158
Ask what is important, what can vary, and
what would make the arrangement

00:27:41.158 --> 00:27:43.858
usable. A cultural brief can prepare those
questions.

00:27:44.318 --> 00:27:48.038
It cannot determine food preferences,
relationships, religious observance, or

00:27:48.038 --> 00:27:50.198
comfort with a setting for someone who has
not been asked.

00:27:50.598 --> 00:27:52.458
There is also a risk in the opposite
direction.

00:27:52.998 --> 00:27:55.998
Calling a concern a cultural preference
can make it sound optional before its

00:27:55.998 --> 00:27:57.078
significance is understood.

00:27:57.078 --> 00:28:01.078
Calling every dissatisfaction a cultural
failure can conceal other problems with

00:28:01.078 --> 00:28:04.938
quality or availability. The stronger
response connects the person's account to

00:28:04.938 --> 00:28:06.378
the actual features of the service.

00:28:06.878 --> 00:28:08.978
What is being offered? What matters to the
person?

00:28:09.318 --> 00:28:12.398
Which conditions can change, and who is
responsible for examining them?

00:28:12.958 --> 00:28:16.018
The stakeholder report also has limits
worth learning from.

00:28:16.538 --> 00:28:20.118
Its authors describe a brief engagement,
meetings held virtually, and

00:28:20.118 --> 00:28:22.498
communities or identities insufficiently
represented.

00:28:23.038 --> 00:28:25.458
These limits do not cancel the concerns
that were raised.

00:28:25.778 --> 00:28:28.778
They tell us whose further knowledge may
be needed before treating the account

00:28:28.778 --> 00:28:32.918
as complete. For staff and leaders, this
creates two responsibilities at once.

00:28:33.298 --> 00:28:35.838
Take an account seriously enough to
understand what it identifies.

00:28:36.318 --> 00:28:39.358
Then distinguish that account from a
conclusion about everyone who shares a

00:28:39.358 --> 00:28:43.118
demographic label. Neither a personal
story nor a broad category should have to

00:28:43.118 --> 00:28:44.458
carry more evidence than it contains.

00:28:44.898 --> 00:28:47.698
The quantitative report's categories and
the stakeholder report's community

00:28:47.698 --> 00:28:49.918
knowledge can therefore challenge each
other productively.

00:28:50.478 --> 00:28:52.818
An apparent group pattern may contain
different experiences.

00:28:53.398 --> 00:28:56.278
A compelling explanation may need to be
checked against service conditions.

00:28:56.818 --> 00:28:59.698
Neither source becomes unnecessary because
the other adds complexity.

00:29:00.238 --> 00:29:01.578
Bring this back to self-determination.

00:29:02.398 --> 00:29:05.258
Evidence about a population can identify
questions for an organization.

00:29:05.738 --> 00:29:08.998
The person's own account remains necessary
to understand a particular choice.

00:29:08.998 --> 00:29:12.758
Information about family or community
relationships should help us ask about

00:29:12.758 --> 00:29:15.298
chosen support, not allocate authority to
someone else.

00:29:15.898 --> 00:29:19.278
A disciplined review can preserve all
three: patterns that deserve attention,

00:29:19.658 --> 00:29:22.558
accounts that reveal possible barriers,
and the person's authority over the

00:29:22.558 --> 00:29:23.598
meaning of a good outcome.

00:29:24.333 --> 00:29:27.073
The community briefs are a central part of
this program.

00:29:27.593 --> 00:29:31.333
They can help us understand migration,
institutions, language, history, and

00:29:31.333 --> 00:29:34.833
sources of belonging. They can also become
misleading if we turn a broad

00:29:34.833 --> 00:29:37.753
description into an expectation of the
next person we meet.

00:29:38.373 --> 00:29:40.193
Begin with the American and Minnesota
briefs.

00:29:40.693 --> 00:29:43.933
Read them with the same curiosity you
would bring to a less familiar community.

00:29:44.413 --> 00:29:47.933
Which habits are described as cultural
when someone else uses them, but as

00:29:47.933 --> 00:29:51.393
common sense when you do? Which American
ideals have supported rights and

00:29:51.393 --> 00:29:55.393
participation, and which interpretations
have made institutions less responsive?

00:29:56.013 --> 00:29:59.093
That examination prepares us to read every
other brief more carefully.

00:29:59.413 --> 00:30:01.813
Then choose a community connected to your
actual work.

00:30:02.253 --> 00:30:03.573
Look for how the account was produced.

00:30:04.153 --> 00:30:08.013
Does it draw on community organizations,
historical records, interviews, survey

00:30:08.013 --> 00:30:09.933
research, or an author's interpretation?

00:30:10.553 --> 00:30:14.033
Does the evidence describe Minnesota,
another place, or a national population?

00:30:14.673 --> 00:30:17.893
When was it gathered? A useful brief makes
those distinctions visible.

00:30:18.393 --> 00:30:21.533
The purpose is to increase the number of
reasonable questions you can ask.

00:30:21.533 --> 00:30:25.313
Reading about family relationships might
prompt you to ask whom the person wants

00:30:25.313 --> 00:30:29.273
involved. Reading about displacement might
help you understand why institutional

00:30:29.273 --> 00:30:30.913
trust cannot simply be assumed.

00:30:31.573 --> 00:30:33.193
Neither reading supplies the person's
answer.

00:30:33.713 --> 00:30:36.173
Research prepares the encounter; it does
not complete it.

00:30:36.583 --> 00:30:39.553
A single label can contain very different
histories.

00:30:39.553 --> 00:30:44.783
Language, generation, disability,
religion, occupation, rural or urban

00:30:44.783 --> 00:30:48.163
experience, and migration can intersect
without producing one predictable

00:30:48.163 --> 00:30:52.503
identity. Two people described by the same
racial category may have different

00:30:52.503 --> 00:30:54.423
relationships with Minnesota institutions.

00:30:55.223 --> 00:30:58.243
Two relatives may differ about what
support would feel respectful.

00:30:58.963 --> 00:31:02.643
Wilder Research's Speaking for Ourselves
study offers a useful example of

00:31:02.643 --> 00:31:07.763
careful limits. The 2015 study included
459 participants from immigrant and

00:31:07.763 --> 00:31:11.123
refugee communities, largely in Hennepin
and Ramsey counties.

00:31:11.743 --> 00:31:15.683
It gathered experiences and perceptions,
and the report explicitly cautions that

00:31:15.683 --> 00:31:18.543
the participants were not statistically
representative of the broader

00:31:18.543 --> 00:31:22.883
communities. Its historical findings can
guide inquiry; they are not a current

00:31:22.883 --> 00:31:25.003
profile of every member of those
communities.

00:31:25.683 --> 00:31:27.783
Some differences need more than a cultural
comparison.

00:31:28.583 --> 00:31:31.803
Tribal Nations have governmental authority
and distinct relationships with the

00:31:31.803 --> 00:31:35.483
state. Learning about Native communities
does not replace the appropriate Tribal

00:31:35.483 --> 00:31:37.403
relations and consultation processes.

00:31:38.203 --> 00:31:41.483
Deaf communities may involve both
disability access and a shared language

00:31:41.483 --> 00:31:45.663
and cultural life. Treating either solely
as a demographic category would miss

00:31:45.663 --> 00:31:49.323
what matters. The practical discipline is
to ask what kind of difference is

00:31:49.323 --> 00:31:50.483
relevant to this decision.

00:31:50.963 --> 00:31:54.863
Is it a personal preference, a language
need, a historical experience, a

00:31:54.863 --> 00:31:57.963
resource constraint, a legal relationship,
or several together?

00:31:58.453 --> 00:32:01.803
That question keeps cultural learning from
becoming a shortcut that hides the

00:32:01.803 --> 00:32:02.903
issue we need to understand.

00:32:03.625 --> 00:32:05.485
Compare two ways of using a brief.

00:32:06.245 --> 00:32:09.445
The first says: this community values
family, so we should direct our

00:32:09.445 --> 00:32:11.065
explanation to the oldest relative.

00:32:11.605 --> 00:32:15.265
The second says: the brief raises
questions about relationships and decision

00:32:15.265 --> 00:32:18.725
support, so we should ask the person whom
they want involved and how.

00:32:26.765 --> 00:32:30.105
The second approach uses cultural
knowledge while preserving the person's

00:32:30.105 --> 00:32:31.945
authority. Now make the task harder.

00:32:31.945 --> 00:32:35.685
Suppose a community organization
recommends a particular way of offering a

00:32:35.685 --> 00:32:38.605
service, while some community members ask
for something different.

00:32:39.105 --> 00:32:42.505
An organization can contribute substantial
knowledge without representing every

00:32:42.505 --> 00:32:44.605
person. Seek the reasons for the
difference.

00:32:45.485 --> 00:32:49.065
Age, role, disability, language, or
service experience may matter.

00:32:49.425 --> 00:32:51.425
So might a disagreement about the service
itself.

00:32:51.965 --> 00:32:55.625
A strong research note records what was
learned, whom the account includes, what

00:32:55.625 --> 00:32:58.145
remains uncertain, and what question will
be asked next.

00:32:58.565 --> 00:33:00.885
It should not become a list of traits
attached to a population.

00:33:00.885 --> 00:33:04.485
When sources conflict, show the
disagreement rather than smoothing it into

00:33:04.485 --> 00:33:05.685
a confident cultural rule.

00:33:06.265 --> 00:33:09.425
In the next stage of this program,
learners will compare their own cultural

00:33:09.425 --> 00:33:12.725
assumptions with a relevant community
brief and then test their questions

00:33:12.725 --> 00:33:13.785
through appropriate engagement.

00:33:14.345 --> 00:33:18.125
That sequence matters. If we only study
other people, our own institutional

00:33:18.125 --> 00:33:20.145
perspective remains the unexamined
standard.

00:33:20.525 --> 00:33:24.065
If we only reflect on ourselves, we may
never learn enough about the people our

00:33:24.065 --> 00:33:26.825
decisions affect. Both forms of learning
are necessary.

00:33:32.458 --> 00:33:35.578
Communication is where many of these
assumptions become visible.

00:33:36.218 --> 00:33:39.698
A person can hear every word and still
understand the situation differently from

00:33:39.698 --> 00:33:43.698
the speaker. The meaning may depend on who
has authority, whether disagreement

00:33:43.698 --> 00:33:47.818
feels possible, how much explanation is
expected, and what has happened before.

00:33:48.518 --> 00:33:51.618
The Water course uses Minnesota
indirectness as one example.

00:33:52.198 --> 00:33:55.038
A phrase can sound approving to one
listener and doubtful to another.

00:33:55.618 --> 00:33:58.598
The useful lesson is not to memorize a
hidden Minnesota dictionary.

00:33:59.098 --> 00:34:02.798
It is to notice that our interpretation is
an interpretation, then make the

00:34:02.798 --> 00:34:03.958
consequential meaning clear.

00:34:04.798 --> 00:34:06.958
Consider a professional who says that an
option should work.

00:34:07.378 --> 00:34:10.838
Does that mean it is available,
recommended, approved, or merely possible?

00:34:11.578 --> 00:34:13.758
A colleague who knows the speaker may fill
in the gap.

00:34:14.218 --> 00:34:16.798
A person new to the system may reasonably
hear something else.

00:34:17.358 --> 00:34:20.878
That gap becomes more consequential when
the speaker controls access to support.

00:34:21.438 --> 00:34:24.378
A clearer response names what is known and
what remains undecided.

00:34:24.958 --> 00:34:27.498
It also invites correction in a way the
person can use.

00:34:28.018 --> 00:34:31.558
Asking whether everything makes sense may
be too broad, particularly when saying

00:34:31.558 --> 00:34:33.118
no feels embarrassing or risky.

00:34:33.698 --> 00:34:36.678
Explaining the options, then checking how
each fits the person's situation,

00:34:36.678 --> 00:34:38.338
gives both people more information.

00:34:38.998 --> 00:34:41.998
The communication task includes the
relationship and the conditions of the

00:34:41.998 --> 00:34:42.398
conversation.

00:34:43.083 --> 00:34:46.563
Clearer wording helps, but it cannot
replace communication access.

00:34:47.283 --> 00:34:51.343
A person may need an interpreter, a
different format, more processing time, a

00:34:51.343 --> 00:34:54.683
quieter setting, or a way of communicating
that the professional has not

00:34:54.683 --> 00:34:58.643
prepared for. Ask about those needs rather
than inferring them from appearance,

00:34:58.943 --> 00:35:00.263
accent, or a community label.

00:35:00.863 --> 00:35:04.323
The National CLAS Standards connect
responsive communication with leadership,

00:35:04.503 --> 00:35:06.803
resources, workforce preparation, and
improvement.

00:35:07.423 --> 00:35:10.783
Their language assistance standards
address offering assistance, communicating

00:35:10.783 --> 00:35:13.503
its availability, and using competent
language support.

00:35:14.103 --> 00:35:17.363
Their organizational standards also
connect community partnership with

00:35:17.363 --> 00:35:21.523
evaluation. The important design lesson is
that communication quality needs an

00:35:21.523 --> 00:35:25.863
institutional foundation. A worker cannot
reliably arrange an interpreter if the

00:35:25.863 --> 00:35:28.283
organization provides no workable booking
process.

00:35:28.843 --> 00:35:32.523
A person cannot exercise an informed
choice if accessible material arrives

00:35:32.523 --> 00:35:36.543
after the decision. An employee cannot
make a meaningful contribution if the

00:35:36.543 --> 00:35:40.143
only acceptable way to disagree is an
unspoken style they have never been

00:35:40.143 --> 00:35:42.703
taught. Those examples require different
responses.

00:35:43.283 --> 00:35:46.843
One concerns service provision, another
the timing of information, and another

00:35:46.843 --> 00:35:47.963
the culture of a workplace.

00:35:48.543 --> 00:35:51.303
A training program should help learners
identify which problem they are

00:35:51.303 --> 00:35:55.023
addressing. Calling all of them cultural
misunderstanding can hide practical

00:35:55.023 --> 00:35:58.703
responsibilities. Calling all of them
technical failures can hide whose needs

00:35:58.703 --> 00:36:00.023
the system was designed around.

00:36:00.543 --> 00:36:03.563
The response needs to account for both
meaning and the conditions that make

00:36:03.563 --> 00:36:04.623
participation possible.

00:36:05.167 --> 00:36:08.287
When an interaction has gone wrong, the
next sentence matters.

00:36:08.847 --> 00:36:12.347
A defensive explanation of good intentions
may leave the impact untouched.

00:36:12.947 --> 00:36:15.107
An apology with no correction may do the
same.

00:36:15.547 --> 00:36:19.287
A more useful response identifies the
misunderstanding, acknowledges its

00:36:19.287 --> 00:36:21.747
consequence, and explains what can happen
next.

00:36:22.247 --> 00:36:25.667
For example, a professional might
recognize that an option was described as

00:36:25.667 --> 00:36:27.427
settled when it was only being considered.

00:36:28.267 --> 00:36:31.607
Repair would include correcting that
impression, explaining the actual choices,

00:36:31.987 --> 00:36:33.927
and checking what the person now wants to
do.

00:36:33.927 --> 00:36:38.227
The wording must fit the situation; the
purpose is to restore usable information

00:36:38.227 --> 00:36:42.147
and decision authority. At work, the same
discipline can make disagreement more

00:36:42.147 --> 00:36:46.307
productive. A supervisor can ask whether a
concern is about the decision itself,

00:36:46.787 --> 00:36:49.247
the evidence supporting it, or how people
were included.

00:36:49.927 --> 00:36:53.127
The answer may reveal that the problem is
more substantial than a difference in

00:36:53.127 --> 00:36:56.887
tone. Attention to cultural context should
increase our ability to hear the

00:36:56.887 --> 00:37:00.347
concern. It should not become a reason to
dismiss the concern as someone's

00:37:00.347 --> 00:37:04.727
communication style. Before moving on, ask
yourself: who in this interaction

00:37:04.727 --> 00:37:08.187
carries the burden of making the meaning
clear, and who bears the consequence

00:37:08.187 --> 00:37:12.387
when it remains unclear? Where the
organization holds greater authority, it

00:37:12.387 --> 00:37:14.787
also has greater capacity to improve those
conditions.

00:37:15.507 --> 00:37:18.547
That brings us to intercultural
development: the ongoing work of

00:37:18.547 --> 00:37:21.967
recognizing a perspective, considering
others, and changing our response when

00:37:21.967 --> 00:37:23.087
the evidence calls for it.

00:37:26.708 --> 00:37:30.688
Consider the statement, people should take
responsibility for their own lives.

00:37:31.008 --> 00:37:35.348
In this illustrative discussion, a staff
member hears respect for agency: people

00:37:35.348 --> 00:37:38.148
should make decisions rather than have
professionals decide for them.

00:37:38.508 --> 00:37:42.428
A community partner hears a possible
transfer of burden: people may be expected

00:37:42.428 --> 00:37:44.768
to navigate barriers the institution
created.

00:37:45.228 --> 00:37:47.228
Both interpretations concern the same
words.

00:37:47.628 --> 00:37:50.228
The difference becomes consequential when
the statement guides a service

00:37:50.228 --> 00:37:54.008
decision. Does personal responsibility
mean honoring someone's priorities and

00:37:54.008 --> 00:37:57.448
choices? Does it mean withdrawing
assistance until someone demonstrates the

00:37:57.448 --> 00:37:58.888
behavior the institution expects?

00:37:59.768 --> 00:38:01.648
The phrase alone cannot answer those
questions.

00:38:02.208 --> 00:38:05.208
Now imagine responding that everyone
ultimately wants the same thing.

00:38:05.428 --> 00:38:06.628
That might express solidarity.

00:38:07.108 --> 00:38:10.148
It might also close down an important
disagreement about what support makes

00:38:10.148 --> 00:38:13.988
choice possible. We need to examine what
the response does in this situation.

00:38:14.388 --> 00:38:17.888
A stronger response would clarify the
decision, identify who bears its burdens,

00:38:18.168 --> 00:38:21.048
and ask the people affected what
meaningful choice would require.

00:38:21.048 --> 00:38:24.888
That inquiry may reveal disagreement
within a community as well as between the

00:38:24.888 --> 00:38:28.728
agency and its partners. Neither side
represents a single cultural viewpoint.

00:38:29.368 --> 00:38:32.108
Intercultural development becomes visible
when we can investigate an

00:38:32.108 --> 00:38:35.408
interpretation, explain our reasoning, and
adjust conduct while remaining

00:38:35.408 --> 00:38:36.668
accountable for the consequences.

00:38:37.548 --> 00:38:40.648
Good intentions matter, but they cannot
establish that a choice was usable.

00:38:41.667 --> 00:38:46.467
The Intercultural Development Inventory,
or IDI, describes five orientations on

00:38:46.467 --> 00:38:50.907
its developmental continuum: Denial,
Polarization, Minimization, Acceptance,

00:38:51.247 --> 00:38:54.547
and Adaptation. These describe different
ways of making sense of cultural

00:38:54.547 --> 00:38:55.827
difference and commonality.

00:38:56.287 --> 00:39:00.227
They are not job titles, racial
identities, or diagnoses we assign after

00:39:00.227 --> 00:39:03.967
hearing one sentence. Bennett's
Developmental Model of Intercultural

00:39:03.967 --> 00:39:07.207
Sensitivity, often called DMIS, is related
but distinct.

00:39:07.727 --> 00:39:09.587
Its six orientations include Integration.

00:39:10.267 --> 00:39:12.547
Integration is not a sixth IDI
orientation.

00:39:13.107 --> 00:39:15.167
Keep that distinction when moving between
resources.

00:39:16.147 --> 00:39:19.387
Formal IDI assessment and individualized
feedback belong with qualified

00:39:19.387 --> 00:39:22.387
administrators. This course does not
produce an IDI score.

00:39:22.787 --> 00:39:24.387
Why does the distinction matter in
practice?

00:39:24.947 --> 00:39:26.407
Because a model should sharpen inquiry.

00:39:26.727 --> 00:39:30.227
If we turn its categories into labels for
colleagues, we can recreate the very

00:39:30.227 --> 00:39:33.507
problem we are studying: treating our
interpretation as the whole person.

00:39:33.987 --> 00:39:35.547
Return to the statement about
responsibility.

00:39:36.267 --> 00:39:39.827
Ask what information each participant
noticed, what they overlooked, and what

00:39:39.827 --> 00:39:41.707
they would need to understand before
changing course.

00:39:42.367 --> 00:39:45.407
A person may recognize cultural
differences yet still lack authority,

00:39:45.667 --> 00:39:48.087
resources, or skill to make the process
accessible.

00:39:48.827 --> 00:39:51.887
Another may use imperfect language while
proposing a useful change.

00:39:52.367 --> 00:39:55.687
We can assess the reasoning and the action
directly, without pretending we know

00:39:55.687 --> 00:39:57.207
someone's developmental orientation.

00:39:57.547 --> 00:39:59.907
That makes feedback specific enough to
improve the work.

00:40:00.792 --> 00:40:05.172
Another framework uses the term cultural
intelligence, or CQ, for four

00:40:05.172 --> 00:40:08.372
capabilities: Drive, Knowledge, Strategy,
and Action.

00:40:09.012 --> 00:40:12.772
In plain language, it asks about
motivation to engage, understanding of

00:40:12.772 --> 00:40:15.852
context, deliberate planning, and the
ability to adjust behavior.

00:40:16.412 --> 00:40:18.592
This is one useful lens within a larger
collection.

00:40:19.392 --> 00:40:22.572
People, Access and Culture uses cultural
intelligence broadly.

00:40:22.572 --> 00:40:27.572
Its curriculum also examines history,
institutions, communication, psychology,

00:40:28.052 --> 00:40:31.032
economics, community knowledge,
disability, and leadership.

00:40:31.812 --> 00:40:35.372
No single framework can tell us whether a
service is equitable or whose

00:40:35.372 --> 00:40:36.992
interpretation should control a decision.

00:40:37.692 --> 00:40:40.032
Try applying complementary questions to
our disagreement.

00:40:41.212 --> 00:40:43.852
Development asks whether we can reconsider
our initial interpretation.

00:40:44.692 --> 00:40:47.892
Communication asks whether the partner's
concern was understood accurately.

00:40:47.892 --> 00:40:51.772
Equity analysis asks who had influence and
who carried the burden of

00:40:51.772 --> 00:40:56.112
participating. Organizational practice
asks who can reopen a decision, provide

00:40:56.112 --> 00:40:57.652
resources, and explain the result.

00:40:58.252 --> 00:41:01.212
What would you change first, and what
evidence would tell you whether the change

00:41:01.212 --> 00:41:03.712
helped? You can pause to work through that
question.

00:41:04.432 --> 00:41:06.912
A defensible answer connects understanding
with action.

00:41:07.372 --> 00:41:10.872
For example, clarify the person's
priorities, identify what support makes

00:41:10.872 --> 00:41:14.252
those choices possible, and ask who has
authority to provide that support.

00:41:14.252 --> 00:41:17.992
Learning has practical force when it
expands the institution's capacity to

00:41:17.992 --> 00:41:20.732
respond. Naming the right framework is
only a beginning.

00:41:24.292 --> 00:41:27.732
The developmental models help us examine
how we make sense of difference.

00:41:28.252 --> 00:41:30.672
They should not become a substitute for
examining the work.

00:41:31.332 --> 00:41:34.352
One useful distinction in the
Intercultural Development Continuum is

00:41:34.352 --> 00:41:37.992
between recognizing cultural patterns and
being able to change perspective and

00:41:37.992 --> 00:41:42.032
behavior appropriately. Understanding that
another view makes sense is not the

00:41:42.032 --> 00:41:43.392
same as knowing what to do next.

00:41:43.872 --> 00:41:47.272
This distinction matters in human services
because several kinds of difficulty

00:41:47.272 --> 00:41:51.092
can look similar. A practitioner may
misunderstand what a person is asking.

00:41:51.092 --> 00:41:54.612
The meaning may be understood but an
appropriate response may be unfamiliar.

00:41:55.232 --> 00:41:58.332
The practitioner may know how to respond
while lacking the necessary authority

00:41:58.332 --> 00:42:00.532
or support. Those are different problems.

00:42:01.112 --> 00:42:04.252
Our instructional implication is to
identify the problem before choosing the

00:42:04.252 --> 00:42:07.312
intervention. More cultural information
may help with one gap.

00:42:08.072 --> 00:42:09.412
Practice and feedback may help with
another.

00:42:09.832 --> 00:42:12.572
A change in organizational capacity may be
needed for a third.

00:42:13.072 --> 00:42:15.852
We should not use a developmental label to
hide that analysis.

00:42:16.392 --> 00:42:19.432
Nor does understanding a perspective
require agreement with every proposed

00:42:19.432 --> 00:42:23.152
action. Staff still have to explain
responsibilities, constraints, and

00:42:23.152 --> 00:42:26.492
reasons. The developmental task is to make
that judgment with a fuller

00:42:26.492 --> 00:42:30.252
understanding of meaning and context, and
to remain able to reconsider it.

00:42:30.612 --> 00:42:33.072
Respectful inquiry and responsible
judgment belong together.

00:42:33.667 --> 00:42:38.087
The CQ framework adds another useful
distinction: knowledge and strategy are

00:42:38.087 --> 00:42:42.107
separate capabilities. Knowing something
about a cultural context does not

00:42:42.107 --> 00:42:44.707
determine how that knowledge should be
used in this encounter.

00:42:45.767 --> 00:42:48.827
Strategy concerns deliberate preparation
and consideration of the situation;

00:42:49.547 --> 00:42:51.487
action concerns the ability to adjust
behavior.

00:42:52.067 --> 00:42:55.147
In this course, we can turn that
distinction into a practical discipline.

00:42:55.667 --> 00:42:58.947
Before an important conversation, identify
the meaning you are taking for

00:42:58.947 --> 00:43:02.507
granted. During it, look for information
that could change your interpretation.

00:43:03.187 --> 00:43:06.267
Afterward, examine whether your response
made the meaning and the available

00:43:06.267 --> 00:43:10.387
choices clearer. This is our application
of the framework, not a CQ assessment.

00:43:10.927 --> 00:43:12.027
The check should be consequential.

00:43:12.827 --> 00:43:15.547
Did an encouraging phrase communicate a
possibility or a commitment?

00:43:16.287 --> 00:43:19.367
Did silence mean agreement, uncertainty,
or a lack of room to disagree?

00:43:19.907 --> 00:43:23.207
The answer requires another person's
account and attention to the conditions of

00:43:23.207 --> 00:43:26.787
the exchange. Preparing several
interpretations is useful only if we

00:43:26.787 --> 00:43:28.047
remain willing to discard them.

00:43:28.047 --> 00:43:31.627
Otherwise, cultural knowledge becomes a
more elaborate way of deciding in

00:43:31.627 --> 00:43:32.667
advance what someone means.

00:43:33.387 --> 00:43:36.627
A strong response explains the relevant
uncertainty and invites correction

00:43:36.627 --> 00:43:39.767
without making the person responsible for
educating the entire organization.

00:43:40.527 --> 00:43:43.307
The professional brings preparation,
accessible communication, and

00:43:43.307 --> 00:43:46.247
accountability for what their words lead
others to reasonably expect.

00:43:47.042 --> 00:43:51.042
Intercultural communication also needs a
standard for judging whether it worked.

00:43:51.382 --> 00:43:55.502
A pleasant exchange, a fluent explanation,
or the professional's confidence is

00:43:55.502 --> 00:43:59.022
insufficient. The important question is
whether people could understand what

00:43:59.022 --> 00:44:02.822
mattered, express a different view, and
participate in the next decision.

00:44:03.422 --> 00:44:06.902
The National CLAS Standards place
communication alongside leadership,

00:44:07.182 --> 00:44:10.322
resources, community partnership, and
ongoing evaluation.

00:44:10.962 --> 00:44:13.302
That organization of the standards is
significant.

00:44:13.302 --> 00:44:17.842
It treats communication as something
institutions must support, rather than a

00:44:17.842 --> 00:44:21.082
personal talent that staff should somehow
supply under any conditions.

00:44:21.742 --> 00:44:25.142
For this program, distinguish the quality
of an explanation from the conditions

00:44:25.142 --> 00:44:28.522
in which it is given. Clear language
cannot compensate for missing

00:44:28.522 --> 00:44:32.562
interpretation. An interpreter cannot
create an option the organization has not

00:44:32.562 --> 00:44:36.502
made available. A worker's patience cannot
resolve an unanswered question that

00:44:36.502 --> 00:44:38.042
only a program owner can decide.

00:44:38.502 --> 00:44:41.042
Those limits are not reasons to stop
improving the encounter.

00:44:41.042 --> 00:44:44.342
They identify where responsibility must
continue beyond it.

00:44:44.982 --> 00:44:47.862
Staff should be able to describe what was
clarified, what remained uncertain,

00:44:48.262 --> 00:44:50.022
and where the unresolved issue was taken.

00:44:50.822 --> 00:44:52.702
Leaders should examine recurring gaps in
that chain.

00:44:53.202 --> 00:44:56.662
Development becomes visible in this work
when a changed interpretation leads to

00:44:56.662 --> 00:44:59.782
a changed response, and the response is
checked from the other person's

00:44:59.782 --> 00:45:03.702
position. The evidence is in the
interaction and its consequences, not the

00:45:03.702 --> 00:45:05.382
vocabulary used to describe competence.

00:45:05.917 --> 00:45:09.977
Consider this constructed workforce
example, not an actual DHS event.

00:45:10.417 --> 00:45:13.837
A supervisor must select an employee to
lead a high visibility project.

00:45:14.517 --> 00:45:16.377
Several people meet the technical
requirements.

00:45:16.857 --> 00:45:20.197
The supervisor favors the person who
speaks quickly in meetings, challenges

00:45:20.197 --> 00:45:22.797
ideas comfortably, and already knows
senior leaders.

00:45:23.317 --> 00:45:24.637
Those behaviors can be useful.

00:45:25.397 --> 00:45:27.557
Have they been connected to the work this
project requires?

00:45:28.337 --> 00:45:32.137
Listening across disagreement, explaining
complex information, organizing

00:45:32.137 --> 00:45:35.317
delivery, and building community
relationships may matter just as much.

00:45:35.317 --> 00:45:39.617
If the selection discussion stops at
confidence or fit, familiar behavior can

00:45:39.617 --> 00:45:41.557
acquire the authority of a performance
standard.

00:45:42.257 --> 00:45:46.337
Now add a second fact. One qualified
employee has repeatedly done the quiet

00:45:46.337 --> 00:45:49.737
work of resolving problems and connecting
colleagues with community expertise.

00:45:50.477 --> 00:45:54.197
That contribution is praised privately but
absent from the selection discussion.

00:45:54.797 --> 00:45:58.137
The question is not whether quiet people
are always overlooked or outspoken

00:45:58.137 --> 00:46:01.557
people are unqualified. It is whether the
evidence being considered actually

00:46:01.557 --> 00:46:03.377
measures the responsibility being
assigned.

00:46:03.377 --> 00:46:07.457
Dominant culture is visible here through
institutional consequences: which

00:46:07.457 --> 00:46:10.857
behavior is recognized, who receives
opportunity, and whose record becomes

00:46:10.857 --> 00:46:12.017
legible to decision makers.

00:46:12.737 --> 00:46:16.137
Race, disability, language, gender, class,
and established relationships can

00:46:16.137 --> 00:46:17.197
interact with those judgments.

00:46:17.997 --> 00:46:21.577
We need evidence about this situation, not
a stereotype about any employee.

00:46:22.167 --> 00:46:26.567
Continue the example. An employee raises a
concern that project opportunities

00:46:26.567 --> 00:46:28.247
keep going to the same small circle.

00:46:28.847 --> 00:46:31.867
Another colleague responds that the team
should avoid making everything about

00:46:31.867 --> 00:46:33.727
race. The conversation moves on.

00:46:34.127 --> 00:46:35.167
What should a leader do next?

00:46:35.567 --> 00:46:38.567
One option is to coach the employee to
raise concerns more tactfully.

00:46:39.107 --> 00:46:41.807
Another is to announce that all future
assignments will rotate.

00:46:42.267 --> 00:46:45.527
A third is to address the dismissal,
examine the allocation of opportunities,

00:46:45.787 --> 00:46:47.767
and clarify what the next decision
requires.

00:46:48.267 --> 00:46:49.807
Before choosing, separate the questions.

00:46:50.347 --> 00:46:53.067
Was a concern shut down? Is there a
pattern in assignments?

00:46:53.667 --> 00:46:56.287
Are selection criteria relevant and
consistently applied?

00:46:56.747 --> 00:46:59.107
Could raising the concern affect the
employee's later opportunities?

00:46:59.767 --> 00:47:02.207
None of these questions is answered by
saying everyone meant well.

00:47:02.707 --> 00:47:05.607
Coaching might support a person's goals,
but making it the only response would

00:47:05.607 --> 00:47:07.867
place the burden on the person who
identified the problem.

00:47:08.487 --> 00:47:12.227
Rotation could broaden access, but it
might bypass necessary qualifications or

00:47:12.227 --> 00:47:13.967
leave the underlying criteria unexplained.

00:47:14.647 --> 00:47:17.167
The third option creates a path to
evidence and accountability.

00:47:17.807 --> 00:47:19.287
Pause if you want to compare those
alternatives.

00:47:19.947 --> 00:47:22.907
The immediate response should keep the
concern open and address the dismissive

00:47:22.907 --> 00:47:26.607
conduct. The next response should examine
decisions, not demand that the

00:47:26.607 --> 00:47:28.927
employee prove an entire institutional
pattern alone.

00:47:34.458 --> 00:47:38.678
A useful review would compare the work
required, the criteria announced, the

00:47:38.678 --> 00:47:42.578
evidence considered, and the opportunities
people had to develop that evidence.

00:47:43.038 --> 00:47:46.878
A manager might discover that informal
assignments created the experience later

00:47:46.878 --> 00:47:47.998
treated as a prerequisite.

00:47:48.558 --> 00:47:52.498
A requirement can appear neutral while
access to meeting it has been uneven.

00:47:53.138 --> 00:47:56.218
The response should therefore reach both
today's decision and tomorrow's

00:47:56.218 --> 00:47:59.518
opportunity. Define relevant criteria
before selection.

00:47:59.978 --> 00:48:01.598
Make developmental assignments visible.

00:48:01.598 --> 00:48:05.158
Explain how community knowledge,
communication, delivery, and technical

00:48:05.158 --> 00:48:06.438
competence will be assessed.

00:48:06.998 --> 00:48:10.518
Provide ways for employees to contribute
evidence that do not depend entirely on

00:48:10.518 --> 00:48:11.518
being noticed in a meeting.

00:48:12.098 --> 00:48:14.178
This does not mean every outcome must be
identical.

00:48:14.758 --> 00:48:17.498
It means differences should be explainable
through the work and a fair

00:48:17.498 --> 00:48:19.158
opportunity to demonstrate readiness.

00:48:19.718 --> 00:48:23.658
Where the concern involves discrimination,
harassment, or retaliation, use the

00:48:23.658 --> 00:48:26.658
appropriate formal process as well as
addressing ordinary management

00:48:26.658 --> 00:48:30.038
responsibilities. A learning exercise
cannot replace that process.

00:48:30.038 --> 00:48:31.978
What would convince you the change worked?

00:48:32.518 --> 00:48:34.238
A new policy document is insufficient.

00:48:34.858 --> 00:48:38.518
Look for whether assignments become
accessible, criteria are actually used,

00:48:38.838 --> 00:48:41.938
concerns can be raised, and employees
recognize an understandable decision

00:48:41.938 --> 00:48:45.438
process. Examine exceptions and unintended
consequences.

00:48:46.238 --> 00:48:49.218
Leadership development belongs in this
course because leaders influence the

00:48:49.218 --> 00:48:50.978
conditions under which others can act.

00:48:51.838 --> 00:48:53.998
Staff can contribute evidence and
challenge assumptions.

00:48:54.478 --> 00:48:57.978
People with authority remain responsible
for decisions, resources, and follow

00:48:57.978 --> 00:48:58.328
through.

00:48:58.708 --> 00:49:02.968
The organizational question is already
present in Minnesota's public record.

00:49:03.428 --> 00:49:06.708
The Department of Health's twenty fourteen
Advancing Health Equity report

00:49:06.708 --> 00:49:10.508
described how accountability arrangements
could favor established organizations

00:49:10.508 --> 00:49:14.428
while treating less familiar, culturally
different organizations with greater

00:49:14.428 --> 00:49:18.688
skepticism. It called for innovation while
retaining responsibility for public

00:49:18.688 --> 00:49:22.748
funds. That tension asks us to examine how
credibility is recognized and

00:49:22.748 --> 00:49:26.288
capacity is built, without assuming that
every new organization is ready.

00:49:26.288 --> 00:49:30.848
Suppose an organization knows a community
well but has limited experience with a

00:49:30.848 --> 00:49:31.988
government's funding procedures.

00:49:32.688 --> 00:49:36.188
A familiar institution may have stronger
administrative systems but weaker

00:49:36.188 --> 00:49:38.488
relationships with the people the program
needs to reach.

00:49:39.108 --> 00:49:40.848
The choice involves real responsibilities.

00:49:41.428 --> 00:49:44.908
We should not pretend that cultural
responsiveness removes financial, quality,

00:49:45.188 --> 00:49:48.808
or safety obligations. We should also ask
whether our method of meeting those

00:49:48.808 --> 00:49:50.828
obligations is the only workable method.

00:49:51.388 --> 00:49:55.008
Could appropriate technical assistance, a
transparent partnership, staged

00:49:55.008 --> 00:49:58.048
responsibilities, or a different
administrative arrangement preserve

00:49:58.048 --> 00:50:00.928
accountability while making community
expertise usable?

00:50:01.528 --> 00:50:03.968
Evaluate such proposals through the
authorized process.

00:50:04.748 --> 00:50:08.148
The lesson is to examine both the standard
and the means of demonstrating it.

00:50:08.808 --> 00:50:10.808
Familiarity can make one approach feel
inevitable.

00:50:11.548 --> 00:50:14.648
Responsible inquiry makes its assumptions
visible and tests whether another

00:50:14.648 --> 00:50:16.588
approach could better serve the public
purpose.

00:50:17.208 --> 00:50:21.428
The twenty twenty one stakeholder feedback
report prepared for DHS's home and

00:50:21.428 --> 00:50:25.068
community based services disparities work
raised themes concerning access,

00:50:25.408 --> 00:50:28.528
relationships with funders, and the limits
of broad racial and ethnic

00:50:28.528 --> 00:50:32.748
categories. Qualitative findings identify
questions to investigate, without

00:50:32.748 --> 00:50:34.968
establishing how often every experience
occurs.

00:50:35.568 --> 00:50:37.728
Imagine applying that lesson to a service
pathway.

00:50:38.128 --> 00:50:41.668
People reach an initial contact but
struggle to obtain a useful next step.

00:50:42.088 --> 00:50:45.568
The first organizational explanation is
that the information has already been

00:50:45.568 --> 00:50:49.768
provided. A closer inquiry could reveal
that information arrives in an unusable

00:50:49.768 --> 00:50:53.528
format, that responsibilities are unclear,
or that the next service is

00:50:53.528 --> 00:50:56.948
unavailable. Different explanations
require different responses.

00:50:57.508 --> 00:51:00.828
Ask people affected what happened, what
they tried, and what would have made the

00:51:00.828 --> 00:51:04.808
next step possible. Examine the
operational evidence alongside those

00:51:04.808 --> 00:51:08.528
accounts. Do not require a person to share
a distressing history to establish

00:51:08.528 --> 00:51:11.148
that a handoff failed. Connect the finding
to authority.

00:51:11.768 --> 00:51:13.468
Frontline staff may improve an
explanation.

00:51:14.048 --> 00:51:15.488
Supervisors may change coordination.

00:51:16.168 --> 00:51:19.228
A program owner may need to change
capacity, funding, or a provider

00:51:19.228 --> 00:51:22.968
arrangement. Community partners may help
shape the response, but should not

00:51:22.968 --> 00:51:25.788
become an unpaid substitute for an
institution's responsibilities.

00:51:26.688 --> 00:51:30.548
Culturally responsive action is specific:
it connects an identified barrier, a

00:51:30.548 --> 00:51:33.788
proposed change, the authority to make it,
and a way to judge its usefulness.

00:51:34.875 --> 00:51:36.655
Now put the response under pressure.

00:51:37.175 --> 00:51:38.695
The agency has limited funds.

00:51:39.335 --> 00:51:40.875
Staff already have full workloads.

00:51:41.155 --> 00:51:44.675
A proposed adaptation could help some
people while creating delays for others.

00:51:45.155 --> 00:51:48.475
These constraints belong in the analysis;
they do not settle the question.

00:51:49.055 --> 00:51:52.515
Compare the likely benefit, burden, cost,
and uncertainty of each option.

00:51:53.055 --> 00:51:55.775
Ask whether the same public purpose can be
achieved through a different

00:51:55.775 --> 00:51:59.535
arrangement. Identify what must remain
consistent, such as a legitimate

00:51:59.535 --> 00:52:03.415
eligibility or safety standard, and what
can change, such as communication,

00:52:03.415 --> 00:52:05.475
timing, location, or support.

00:52:06.195 --> 00:52:09.755
Verify applicable requirements rather than
assuming every familiar practice is

00:52:09.755 --> 00:52:13.675
mandatory. A credible commitment names a
responsible owner, the resources

00:52:13.675 --> 00:52:15.915
needed, and the people who can authorize
the work.

00:52:16.295 --> 00:52:19.295
It specifies what staff should do
differently and when the change will be

00:52:19.295 --> 00:52:22.875
reviewed. It explains what cannot
currently change and why, allowing

00:52:22.875 --> 00:52:26.655
appropriate challenge. For evaluation,
distinguish delivery from usefulness.

00:52:27.535 --> 00:52:30.295
Staff may complete a new step while people
still cannot use the service.

00:52:30.835 --> 00:52:33.315
Look at the process and ask affected
people about the result.

00:52:34.035 --> 00:52:35.635
Consider who remains absent from the
evidence.

00:52:36.275 --> 00:52:38.275
If the response does not help, revise it.

00:52:38.615 --> 00:52:39.675
That is part of accountability.

00:52:40.355 --> 00:52:43.755
The organization's willingness to learn
becomes meaningful when it changes a

00:52:43.755 --> 00:52:47.375
condition that people actually encounter,
rather than simply improving the

00:52:47.375 --> 00:52:48.555
explanation of that condition.

00:52:54.208 --> 00:52:56.348
Here is a final constructed decision.

00:52:56.868 --> 00:53:00.448
A program has resources for one initial
improvement to the way people obtain

00:53:00.448 --> 00:53:04.628
help. Staff recommend a single central
contact because it could reduce confusion

00:53:04.628 --> 00:53:06.088
and make responsibility clearer.

00:53:06.708 --> 00:53:09.748
Community partners recommend support
through trusted local organizations

00:53:09.748 --> 00:53:12.888
because some people are not reaching or
trusting the central route.

00:53:13.408 --> 00:53:16.628
Others warn that a local arrangement could
create uneven availability and

00:53:16.628 --> 00:53:19.728
unclear accountability. Each
recommendation protects something

00:53:19.728 --> 00:53:21.188
important and carries a risk.

00:53:21.708 --> 00:53:25.128
Central coordination may simplify the
agency's work without making access

00:53:25.128 --> 00:53:28.608
easier. Local support may strengthen
relationships while leaving people

00:53:28.608 --> 00:53:31.208
dependent on where they live or which
organization they know.

00:53:31.868 --> 00:53:35.068
Your task is to recommend an initial
approach and state what evidence could

00:53:35.068 --> 00:53:35.908
change your recommendation.

00:53:36.868 --> 00:53:39.848
Start with the people affected, not the
preferred organizational model.

00:53:40.328 --> 00:53:41.428
What happens when they seek help?

00:53:41.808 --> 00:53:42.968
Which barriers are documented?

00:53:43.408 --> 00:53:44.488
Whose experiences are missing?

00:53:45.068 --> 00:53:47.548
What responsibilities and standards must
the arrangement preserve?

00:53:48.168 --> 00:53:49.588
Next examine the cultural assumptions.

00:53:50.188 --> 00:53:52.328
Are we equating a uniform route with fair
access?

00:53:52.928 --> 00:53:56.088
Are we treating a trusted community
organization as if it speaks for everyone?

00:53:56.728 --> 00:53:59.928
Are we assuming that people should adapt
to the institution, or that adaptation

00:53:59.928 --> 00:54:02.228
has no costs? Choose your next step.

00:54:02.768 --> 00:54:04.928
You may pause for further analysis before
the debrief.

00:54:10.583 --> 00:54:14.043
A recommendation might combine an
accountable central function with a

00:54:14.043 --> 00:54:16.083
limited partnership that helps people use
it.

00:54:16.283 --> 00:54:19.103
But adding both elements is not
automatically better.

00:54:19.643 --> 00:54:23.843
Without defined roles, resources, and
communication, a hybrid could create

00:54:23.843 --> 00:54:25.083
another confusing handoff.

00:54:25.543 --> 00:54:27.783
A stronger recommendation explains the
mechanism.

00:54:28.343 --> 00:54:32.023
For example, preserve one responsible
program owner while testing whether an

00:54:32.023 --> 00:54:35.643
authorized community partnership helps
people complete a specific step they

00:54:35.643 --> 00:54:36.743
currently cannot complete.

00:54:36.743 --> 00:54:41.203
Define what the partner can do, what
remains the agency's responsibility, and

00:54:41.203 --> 00:54:43.223
how people can obtain help without that
partner.

00:54:43.863 --> 00:54:46.683
Include the people affected in judging
whether the arrangement is useful.

00:54:47.343 --> 00:54:50.523
Another defensible recommendation could
prioritize fixing the central route

00:54:50.523 --> 00:54:54.023
first, if evidence shows that the central
barrier affects people across

00:54:54.023 --> 00:54:55.763
communities and can be changed promptly.

00:54:56.203 --> 00:54:58.583
That choice must still identify who
remains excluded.

00:54:59.103 --> 00:55:02.083
The point is to show how evidence supports
the choice and where uncertainty

00:55:02.083 --> 00:55:04.863
remains. Assess your reasoning against six
questions.

00:55:04.863 --> 00:55:06.903
Did you separate evidence from inference?

00:55:07.283 --> 00:55:08.723
Identify an institutional assumption?

00:55:09.143 --> 00:55:12.143
Include affected people's perspectives
without treating them as interchangeable?

00:55:12.883 --> 00:55:14.283
Propose a meaningful adaptation?

00:55:14.763 --> 00:55:15.983
Locate authority and resources?

00:55:16.563 --> 00:55:17.883
Explain how you would check the effect?

00:55:25.643 --> 00:55:28.763
A response can sound respectful and still
fail these tests.

00:55:29.223 --> 00:55:32.863
A strong answer names a practical change,
acknowledges its limits, and remains

00:55:32.863 --> 00:55:35.183
open to correction by evidence and
experience.

00:55:35.792 --> 00:55:39.472
The Water We Swim In is an entryway to
People, Access and Culture.

00:55:40.132 --> 00:55:43.712
The American, Minnesota, and European
American briefs help us examine the

00:55:43.712 --> 00:55:45.592
institution's own cultural inheritance.

00:55:46.252 --> 00:55:49.592
The community collection extends that
inquiry through histories, languages,

00:55:50.052 --> 00:55:53.412
relationships, disability experiences, and
local knowledge.

00:55:53.852 --> 00:55:57.172
Read those materials to prepare better
questions, then learn from the people

00:55:57.172 --> 00:56:00.512
involved. The intercultural curriculum
develops interpretation and

00:56:00.512 --> 00:56:04.252
communication. Developmental frameworks
support reflection without becoming

00:56:04.252 --> 00:56:08.252
labels for colleagues. CLAS learning
connects communication with organizational

00:56:08.252 --> 00:56:13.192
capacity. Leadership learning examines
authority, opportunity, disagreement, and

00:56:13.192 --> 00:56:17.492
responsibility. The equity tools help turn
analysis into an action that can be

00:56:17.492 --> 00:56:20.752
examined and improved. Choose your next
learning step around a real

00:56:20.752 --> 00:56:24.912
responsibility. If you supervise, examine
a decision about opportunity or

00:56:24.912 --> 00:56:28.472
credibility. If you support people
directly, study a barrier between an

00:56:28.472 --> 00:56:30.212
offered service and a usable service.

00:56:30.212 --> 00:56:35.092
If you design policy, manage resources, or
coordinate partners, examine which

00:56:35.092 --> 00:56:37.332
assumptions shape the options available to
others.

00:56:38.012 --> 00:56:41.692
Bring back a specific piece of work: the
decision, the assumption you examined,

00:56:42.032 --> 00:56:45.472
the evidence you used, the perspectives
still missing, and the change you

00:56:45.472 --> 00:56:48.692
recommend. Identify who can act and how
the result will be checked.

00:56:49.092 --> 00:56:52.552
The practical standard is whether deeper
understanding changes judgment, whether

00:56:52.552 --> 00:56:55.552
judgment changes work, and whether people
experience a more responsive

00:56:55.552 --> 00:56:58.712
institution. That is the continuing
purpose of this collection.

00:56:59.250 --> 00:57:03.190
Before closing, consider what it means to
make community knowledge part of the

00:57:03.190 --> 00:57:07.630
institution's work. A collection of
observations can inform a decision without

00:57:07.630 --> 00:57:11.270
giving the people affected any continuing
role in how that decision is made.

00:57:11.950 --> 00:57:15.730
Consultation, partnership, and governance
should not be used as interchangeable

00:57:15.730 --> 00:57:19.530
terms. The twenty fourteen Department of
Health report connected stronger

00:57:19.530 --> 00:57:22.250
relationships with participation in
governance and oversight.

00:57:22.750 --> 00:57:26.070
It also proposed involving affected
stakeholders throughout grant development.

00:57:26.070 --> 00:57:30.010
These recommendations go beyond asking
communities to explain themselves after

00:57:30.010 --> 00:57:31.790
the institution has chosen its direction.

00:57:32.450 --> 00:57:35.070
The implication for our program is to make
authority explicit.

00:57:35.850 --> 00:57:37.390
Which questions can people help define?

00:57:37.830 --> 00:57:39.170
Which alternatives remain open?

00:57:39.630 --> 00:57:41.170
Who decides how evidence is weighed?

00:57:41.630 --> 00:57:44.270
What happens when community advice
conflicts with an organizational

00:57:44.270 --> 00:57:48.430
preference? Clear answers allow people to
judge whether the invitation offers

00:57:48.430 --> 00:57:52.530
meaningful influence. They also protect
against a different mistake: treating a

00:57:52.530 --> 00:57:55.350
community partner as a substitute for all
affected people.

00:57:55.350 --> 00:58:00.050
Expertise, trusted relationships, and
representational authority are related but

00:58:00.050 --> 00:58:03.570
distinct. A strong relationship can reveal
important knowledge while still

00:58:03.570 --> 00:58:05.130
leaving other experiences unheard.

00:58:05.990 --> 00:58:08.770
Staff and leaders need to preserve both
contribution and accountability.

00:58:09.530 --> 00:58:11.910
The organization remains answerable for
its decisions.

00:58:12.610 --> 00:58:15.590
Community involvement should make the
reasons and consequences more visible,

00:58:15.590 --> 00:58:18.610
rather than obscure who is responsible
when an arrangement fails.

00:58:19.333 --> 00:58:23.393
An organization can agree with this
approach and still leave staff unable to

00:58:23.393 --> 00:58:27.333
use it. The distance between agreement and
action is often the distance between

00:58:27.333 --> 00:58:29.373
an aspiration and an operating commitment.

00:58:29.993 --> 00:58:33.733
Someone needs authority to decide, time to
work, and resources appropriate to

00:58:33.733 --> 00:58:38.353
the change. CLAS makes allocated resources
and management accountability part of

00:58:38.353 --> 00:58:42.473
organizational practice. Our instructional
implication is to examine the support

00:58:42.473 --> 00:58:46.133
behind a commitment, rather than measure
commitment only by the language in

00:58:46.133 --> 00:58:49.353
which it is announced. This also changes
how we discuss constraints.

00:58:50.113 --> 00:58:53.053
A legitimate limit should be explained
precisely enough to distinguish it from

00:58:53.053 --> 00:58:57.073
habit. Is the obstacle a requirement, a
capacity shortage, an unresolved

00:58:57.073 --> 00:58:59.733
division of responsibility, or a choice
about priorities?

00:59:00.213 --> 00:59:03.153
Those conditions may require different
owners and different responses.

00:59:03.913 --> 00:59:07.293
Verification matters before describing any
familiar arrangement as mandatory.

00:59:07.833 --> 00:59:09.733
The same precision should apply to
adaptation.

00:59:10.353 --> 00:59:13.533
State what would change, whose
participation it would enable, what burden

00:59:13.533 --> 00:59:16.433
it might create elsewhere, and what
evidence supports trying it.

00:59:16.953 --> 00:59:20.333
Cultural responsiveness does not remove
the need to consider quality, safety,

00:59:20.713 --> 00:59:22.113
cost, or fairness to other people.

00:59:22.853 --> 00:59:25.133
It changes the range of knowledge used to
make that judgment.

00:59:25.713 --> 00:59:29.093
Community experience belongs alongside
operational information when deciding

00:59:29.093 --> 00:59:32.533
which costs matter, whose burdens have
been overlooked, and what a workable

00:59:32.533 --> 00:59:33.493
improvement would look like.

00:59:33.833 --> 00:59:36.093
Who can judge whether the response was
useful?

00:59:36.633 --> 00:59:40.193
An institution can verify that an activity
occurred while people still encounter

00:59:40.193 --> 00:59:43.273
the original difficulty. Both findings may
be accurate.

00:59:43.813 --> 00:59:47.193
The twenty twenty one stakeholder report
ends by calling for a clear connection

00:59:47.193 --> 00:59:49.273
between community input and program
improvement.

00:59:49.753 --> 00:59:53.513
It also identifies further work needed on
geography and differences within

00:59:53.513 --> 00:59:54.933
racial and ethnic categories.

00:59:55.553 --> 00:59:59.673
Its conclusion gives us a responsibility
to continue inquiry, not permission to

00:59:59.673 --> 01:00:01.773
treat one consultation as a complete
picture.

01:00:02.253 --> 01:00:05.633
Our evaluation should therefore connect
the action to the experience it was

01:00:05.633 --> 01:00:09.473
meant to change. Ask what became more
understandable, usable, or open to

01:00:09.473 --> 01:00:12.813
influence. Examine who did not benefit or
could not participate.

01:00:13.493 --> 01:00:16.473
Preserve differences in accounts rather
than averaging them into a reassuring

01:00:16.473 --> 01:00:20.313
description. If the evidence remains
mixed, say what is known and what needs

01:00:20.313 --> 01:00:23.773
another response. Learning is not
disproved by finding an unresolved

01:00:23.773 --> 01:00:27.793
problem. It loses practical value when the
organization uses completion of

01:00:27.793 --> 01:00:31.533
training or consultation to close a
question the evidence has left open.

01:00:32.053 --> 01:00:35.033
People, Access and Culture brings these
responsibilities together.

01:00:35.553 --> 01:00:37.533
Examine the institution's inherited lens.

01:00:37.973 --> 01:00:39.113
Learn the community context.

01:00:39.613 --> 01:00:41.213
Ask the person. Locate authority.

01:00:41.773 --> 01:00:45.133
Test the response. The standard is a more
defensible judgment and a more

01:00:45.133 --> 01:00:48.233
responsive institution, experienced by the
people its work affects.
